WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:10.160
Across the infinite cosmos of the video game
‘No Man’s Sky,’ there are a similarly

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:13.650
infinite number of alien lifeforms.

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:19.200
Through the use of procedural generation,
the game contains more than 18 quintillion

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:24.310
planets to discover — many of which teem
with extraordinary creatures.

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:29.270
While No Man’s Sky had a rocky reception
at launch, the developers have done persistent

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:32.870
work making this virtual universe worth exploring.

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:40.410
So, for this entry into the archive, we’ll
embark on a classic documentary-style expedition,

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:46.710
exploring the lives of extraterrestrials on
scorching worlds, frozen wastelands, and many

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:47.710
others.

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:52.420
I’ll also speculate on how the biology of
each lifeform might feasibly work.

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:58.360
I admit that there are a few too many total
planets for this Archivist to document, so

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:03.570
this documentary will focus on only the most
interesting ones I’ve discovered.

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:10.579
Now, let’s set off into the unknown, and
see what awaits us among the stars…

00:01:10.579 --> 00:01:16.990
We’ll begin with an earth-like, temperate
world located a comfortable distance from

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:18.499
its host star.

00:01:18.499 --> 00:01:23.990
Since we are the first explorers to ever witness
this planet, it’s up to us to come up with

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:24.990
a name.

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:30.350
We’ll call this ringed jewel of the cosmos
Planet Rhea — a land abundant with the promise

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:32.040
of discovery.

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:37.770
Entering the atmosphere near the equator,
we can see a vast landscape of green fields

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:39.549
stretching before us.

00:01:39.549 --> 00:01:46.389
Under the orbiting rings above, who knows
what extraterrestrial life has emerged?

00:01:46.389 --> 00:01:50.670
Stepping out of the spacecraft, I spot something
moving in the distance.

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:56.389
A herd of herbivorous giants are grazing on
the local vegetation.

00:01:56.389 --> 00:01:59.679
These newly-discovered creatures also need
a name.

00:01:59.679 --> 00:02:01.319
We’ll call them…

00:02:01.319 --> 00:02:03.579
Titanomagnus Magnificus.

00:02:03.579 --> 00:02:06.079
Which might be a bit much, but I’m excited.

00:02:06.079 --> 00:02:11.750
The Titanomagnus seem to have converged upon
a niche similar to sauropod dinosaurs from

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:12.970
Earth’s past.

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:18.250
They survive by being too large for most predators
to bother with, and sustain their bulk by

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:22.680
vacuuming up over one ton of plant matter
each day.

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:28.470
With their long necks and curious ears, the
Titanomagnus actually resemble another earth

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:34.370
animal: the giraffe, a lifeform whose long
neck helps them reach leaves at the tops of

00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:35.370
trees.

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:42.069
Yet there are no trees in this region of Planet
Rhea, so why bother with such a long neck?

00:02:42.069 --> 00:02:47.569
Perhaps a tree-like genus recently went extinct…
or perhaps the necks serve as watchtowers

00:02:47.569 --> 00:02:51.630
to keep an eye out for potential dangers.

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:53.670
And speaking of potential dangers.

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:57.030
A huge, armored predator is on the move.

00:02:57.030 --> 00:03:03.250
I’ll call these unfriendly customers the
Arachnomantis, because, well, I can.

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:09.209
The Arachnomantis possess a bone crushing
beak, and seem to have some kind of exoskeleton.

00:03:09.209 --> 00:03:16.129
The biological differences between the Arachnomantis
and Titanomagnus seem so extreme, I wonder

00:03:16.129 --> 00:03:21.569
if — like land vertebrates and insects here
on Earth — these two terrestrial lineages

00:03:21.569 --> 00:03:24.980
split long before crawling onto land.

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:30.440
Yet unlike insects, the Arachnomantis are
megafauna in their own right, and a serious

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:32.760
danger upon these plains.

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:39.349
With our different evolutionary origins, it’s
likely I wouldn’t be very tasty to an Arachnomantis…

00:03:39.349 --> 00:03:42.859
but it’s best not to test that theory.

00:03:42.859 --> 00:03:48.079
For the next leg of our voyage across Planet
Rhea, we’ll be traveling in a terrestrial

00:03:48.079 --> 00:03:50.599
exocraft called a Roamer.

00:03:50.599 --> 00:03:56.389
This vehicle will offer protection as we continue
to explore this unfamiliar land.

00:03:56.389 --> 00:04:01.629
Plus, it’s nice to not have to walk for
the entire voyage.

00:04:01.629 --> 00:04:05.940
Not far from our Roamer, something is disturbing
the grass.

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:08.430
This is a Crested Rhealope.

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:12.569
These diminutive grazers are difficult to
get a glimpse of, as they’ve learned their

00:04:12.569 --> 00:04:17.250
best chance of survival in this world of giants
is to stay out of sight.

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:22.150
Their skittish behavior likens them to the
antelopes of the grasslands here on Earth.

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:26.820
We’re lucky to be able to get a sighting
of such a shy creature.

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:31.640
But Rhealope aren’t the only things hiding
in the grass.

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:35.920
Even more elusive are the shadowy Stripped
Dreadtooth.

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:39.950
These cat-like hunters are exceedingly difficult
to spot.

00:04:39.950 --> 00:04:44.910
Like tigers, their unique pattern of stripes
help them blend into their environment, and

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:48.290
the spines on their back help them disappear
even further.

00:04:48.290 --> 00:04:51.700
And this Dreadtooth has caught the scent of
something.

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:57.050
Typically, Dreadtooth feed only on Rhealopes,
and the youngest of Titanomagnus.

00:04:57.050 --> 00:05:00.280
But this Dreadtooth has its sights on an adult.

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:02.960
We might have a serious fight on our hands.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:05.250
The Dreadtooth circles in close…

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:07.480
then seems to think better of it.

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:11.770
A full grown Titanomagnus isn’t exactly
an easy target.

00:05:11.770 --> 00:05:14.290
It’s just not worth the risk.

00:05:14.290 --> 00:05:21.970
So, the Dreadtooth returns to the prowl, looking
for something a little smaller to snack on...

00:05:21.970 --> 00:05:25.170
The sun is now setting on Planet Rhea.

00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:27.910
Yet with the night, comes a final surprise.

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:33.020
The Arachnomantis, while a fearsome predator
during the daylight, lights up the dark with

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:37.990
a bioluminescent display as it calls out to
others of its kind.

00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:43.870
This ritual, which might be for mating purposes,
is a touching one for such a deadly creature.

00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:50.810
We’ve seen much during our travels across
planet Rhea, but now, the stars call us once

00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:51.810
again.

00:05:51.810 --> 00:06:00.300
This world is just one of many, and the moment
has come to continue our expedition…

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:06.520
The next stop on our voyage, planet Boreas,
lies on the outer edge of its star’s habitable

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:08.100
zone.

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:12.800
Entering the atmosphere, I find a land trapped
in a never-ending winter.

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:18.520
Boreas’s position, combined with a dim host
star, means temperature on the surface rarely

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:24.100
rises above freezing, and a layer of snow
and ice covers the surface year-round.

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:28.970
It’s not the sort of place one might expect
to find life…

00:06:28.970 --> 00:06:31.370
Yet life is here all the same.

00:06:31.370 --> 00:06:37.140
This is a Snowsnuffler, a lifeform perfectly
adapted for a sub-zero lifestyle.

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:42.740
Large, lumbering herbivores, they’re protected
from the cold thanks to a thick layer of fat

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:44.340
and insulating fur.

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:49.080
Wrapping oneself in the biological equivalent
of a thick winter coat is one of the most

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:53.930
common ways that animals here on Earth have
been able to conquer cold environments.

00:06:53.930 --> 00:07:00.180
So, it makes sense the Snowsnufflers would
have a convergent tactic for staying warm.

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:03.470
But surviving the elements is only half the
battle.

00:07:03.470 --> 00:07:09.060
To persist on planet Boreas, the Snowsnufflers
must also find food.

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:13.000
Luckily, this is something the Snowsnuffler
is quite good at.

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:18.340
Their sensitive trunks are equipped with scent
receptors powerful enough to locate the subterranean

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:21.030
vegetation the Snowsnufflers feed upon.

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:28.300
So, the Snowsnufflers have sniffed their way
into an enviable spot on the food-web.

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:32.360
But there’s more than one way to survive
on a planet of ice.

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:37.160
Horned Chillmanders are scaly creatures that
aren’t as well insulated from the cold,

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:40.490
and aren’t as proficient at sniffing out
food.

00:07:40.490 --> 00:07:46.590
Instead, they have a different strategy to
confront the low temperatures — hibernation.

00:07:46.590 --> 00:07:51.720
On Earth, numerous animals hibernate during
winter months, slowing their metabolic rate

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:56.550
to conserve energy — only to emerge again
when food is more abundant.

00:07:56.550 --> 00:08:01.870
On planet Boreas, however, temperatures drop
without warning year-round — which is why

00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:07.560
the Chillmanders are capable of slowing their
metabolic rate multiple times per year.

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:13.050
These lethargic lifeforms spend most of their
time basking in the sun, expending precious

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:15.430
little energy even when not hibernating.

00:08:15.430 --> 00:08:21.810
It’s a lifestyle I’m a bit jealous of,
even if it’s not the most exciting.

00:08:21.810 --> 00:08:28.500
Yet where there are herbivores — particularly
abundant, slow-moving ones — there are invariably

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:29.680
carnivores.

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:35.240
Lumbermaws are tall, somewhat awkward looking
predators that spend most of their time reared

00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:39.890
up on their hind legs to intimidate rivals
and survey the landscape.

00:08:39.890 --> 00:08:45.930
While they are the dominant predators of this
region, Lumbermaws aren’t particularly aggressive.

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:52.360
Like many species of bears, they are hypocarnivorous,
meaning their diet consists mostly of fungi,

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:55.900
berries, and other plant materials rather
than meat.

00:08:55.900 --> 00:09:01.400
After all, on a planet as harsh as Boreas,
being picky just isn’t practical.

00:09:01.400 --> 00:09:05.830
I’m not entirely certain if they’d like
the taste of me, so I’ll give them some

00:09:05.830 --> 00:09:08.280
personal space.

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:14.010
Planet Boreas is an extreme and fascinating
world, but the time has come to continue our

00:09:14.010 --> 00:09:18.280
voyage…

00:09:18.280 --> 00:09:25.670
Our next galactic destination is Planet Nereus,
home to an incredible planetwide ocean.

00:09:25.670 --> 00:09:31.610
Entering the atmosphere, I find that only
a few small islands break up the endless expanse

00:09:31.610 --> 00:09:32.860
of water.

00:09:32.860 --> 00:09:37.070
Most life on Planet Nereus is likely waiting
under the waves.

00:09:37.070 --> 00:09:43.070
Yet touching down on one of the larger islands,
I find some intrepid species have come ashore.

00:09:43.070 --> 00:09:46.640
At the water’s edge, I spot a coastal Shellswimmer.

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:52.420
Amphibious herbivores, Shellswimmers feed
off marine plants that grow in the shallows

00:09:52.420 --> 00:09:53.830
just beyond the shore.

00:09:53.830 --> 00:09:59.610
It’s a lifestyle similar to the Marine Iguanas
of the Galápagos Islands, which likewise

00:09:59.610 --> 00:10:03.460
spend most of their lifespan in the intertidal
zone.

00:10:03.460 --> 00:10:09.140
On Planet Nereus, even land-dwellers are tied
to the sea.

00:10:09.140 --> 00:10:14.840
Elsewhere on the island, the bizarre Glowhorns
feed on coastal swamp-grass.

00:10:14.840 --> 00:10:20.200
In appearance and behavior, Glowhorns are
convergent with hoofed ruminant mammals like

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:21.200
deer.

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:26.150
Yet when night falls, these lifeforms reveal
a side that is all their own.

00:10:26.150 --> 00:10:31.390
Bioluminescent display structures on their
back light up, creating a dazzling display

00:10:31.390 --> 00:10:35.130
that helps the Glowhorns find potential mates
in the darkness.

00:10:35.130 --> 00:10:39.250
It’s a mesmerizing spectacle to see in person.

00:10:39.250 --> 00:10:44.090
Yet the light of Glowhorns can attract more
than potential mates.

00:10:44.090 --> 00:10:46.300
It can also attract predators.

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:51.560
And on the island coast, no predator is more
dangerous than the Terrorbeak.

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:57.980
These t-rex-like creatures are huge, towering
over all other land animals on these islands.

00:10:57.980 --> 00:11:04.480
It might seem surprising to see large creatures
in an isolated ecosystem, yet this is actually

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:06.750
an example of Island Gigantism.

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:13.370
A trend that exists on Earth as well in species
like Komodo Dragons, Island Gigantism is a

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:19.910
biological phenomenon in which a species on
an island increases dramatically in size.

00:11:19.910 --> 00:11:26.990
For the Terrorbeaks, their massive bulk allows
them to feed on any species they wish…

00:11:26.990 --> 00:11:31.890
Yet land animals make up only a small percentage
of life on Nereus.

00:11:31.890 --> 00:11:36.700
Diving under the waves, I find a much denser
ecosystem awaits.

00:11:36.700 --> 00:11:42.820
The first lifeforms I spot are the inquisitive,
tentacled Cephalophores, that move in sporadic

00:11:42.820 --> 00:11:46.110
patterns in accordance to the ocean currents.

00:11:46.110 --> 00:11:51.570
Cephalophores travel in groups, perhaps for
protection, or perhaps because they just like

00:11:51.570 --> 00:11:52.610
the company.

00:11:52.610 --> 00:11:58.110
Their soft bodies and manipulatory tentacles
resemble those of an octopus — and like

00:11:58.110 --> 00:12:01.089
octopuses, they’re quite intelligent.

00:12:01.089 --> 00:12:06.990
Exactly how intelligent is difficult to measure,
but they show an acute sense of curiosity,

00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:08.810
circling around me as I swim by.

00:12:08.810 --> 00:12:15.760
I wonder if perhaps the Cephalophores feel
they are studying me.

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:20.880
The bulk of underwater species within the
planet-wide ocean are fast moving, fish-like

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:23.400
lifeforms we’ll call Silverfins.

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:28.740
They gather in schools that number in the
thousands, much like the true fish of our

00:12:28.740 --> 00:12:29.740
oceans.

00:12:29.740 --> 00:12:36.000
And with such high numbers, Silverfins are
an appealing food source for aquatic predators.

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:39.880
And one such predator is lurking below.

00:12:39.880 --> 00:12:44.250
This silent, streamlined hunter is a Saltwater
Reefstalker.

00:12:44.250 --> 00:12:50.220
The premier hunter of this global ocean, Reefstalkers
are significantly faster than their preferred

00:12:50.220 --> 00:12:55.350
prey — using their muscular tail to propel
themselves through the water.

00:12:55.350 --> 00:13:01.490
Their torpedo-like body plan mirrors that
of most sharks, as does their killer appetite.

00:13:01.490 --> 00:13:05.600
In these bountiful waters, every day is a
feast.

00:13:05.600 --> 00:13:11.560
Yet fortunately, like Earth sharks, this Reefstalker
ultimately isn’t that interested in taking

00:13:11.560 --> 00:13:12.850
a bite out of me.

00:13:12.850 --> 00:13:17.800
All the same, I’d feel safer with some extra
protection.

00:13:17.800 --> 00:13:23.840
To explore the deepest region of this global
ocean, I’m traveling in an aquatic exocraft

00:13:23.840 --> 00:13:24.840
called a Nautilon.

00:13:24.840 --> 00:13:31.270
And at the bottom of the largest trench of
Planet Nereus, something mysterious is waiting.

00:13:31.270 --> 00:13:37.170
A bright light emanates from the eye of a
strange organism growing from a wall.

00:13:37.170 --> 00:13:43.399
Exiting my submarine, I move in closer, only
to be caught in a hypnotic vortex of light.

00:13:43.399 --> 00:13:46.670
It takes all my strength to break away.

00:13:46.670 --> 00:13:52.370
That was an Abyssal Horror, a lifeform that
calls to prey with a bioluminescent lure,

00:13:52.370 --> 00:13:54.590
much like an Earth angler fish.

00:13:54.590 --> 00:14:01.620
I suddenly feel that I have seen enough of
planet Nereus…

00:14:01.620 --> 00:14:05.779
Racing along through the stars, I find my
ship is low on fuel.

00:14:05.779 --> 00:14:09.720
Fortunately, I know just the place to get
some more.

00:14:09.720 --> 00:14:15.020
This strange geometric construction is a space
station — a common sight in this part of

00:14:15.020 --> 00:14:16.020
the cosmos.

00:14:16.020 --> 00:14:22.540
They function almost like intergalactic truck
stops, and are open to all kinds of space-faring

00:14:22.540 --> 00:14:23.540
life.

00:14:23.540 --> 00:14:28.120
As our ship enters the space station through
the main aperture, the automatic guidance

00:14:28.120 --> 00:14:32.830
system takes over, and we’re ushered towards
the first available landing pad.

00:14:32.830 --> 00:14:38.930
After so much time exploring the cosmic wilds,
it’s a strange sensation to return to an

00:14:38.930 --> 00:14:39.930
artificial environment.

00:14:39.930 --> 00:14:45.730
Yet we need fuel to continue our expedition,
so we might as well relax.

00:14:45.730 --> 00:14:50.980
This station is run by the diminutive Gek
— one of the primary sentient species in

00:14:50.980 --> 00:14:52.610
this region of space.

00:14:52.610 --> 00:14:58.110
They’re organized and rather pragmatic beings,
more interested in practical matters than

00:14:58.110 --> 00:14:59.110
conversation.

00:14:59.110 --> 00:15:04.170
While I’d like to know more about the specifics
of their biology, I’ve yet to meet a Gek

00:15:04.170 --> 00:15:08.620
comfortable with discussing that topic, so
I won’t press it.

00:15:08.620 --> 00:15:12.970
All sorts of different species pass through
this particular station.

00:15:12.970 --> 00:15:18.240
Some, like the mysterious Korvax, appear to
be entirely artificial.

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:24.170
How these inorganic lifeforms function is
of particular interest, but like the Gek,

00:15:24.170 --> 00:15:26.910
they aren’t keen on sharing secrets.

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:32.110
Still, just being in the presence of other
intelligent lifeforms — some of which are

00:15:32.110 --> 00:15:35.680
likely explorers themselves — is a special
privilege.

00:15:35.680 --> 00:15:43.050
But with the ship refueled, the time is right
to return to our voyage…

00:15:43.050 --> 00:15:48.360
The second to last planet we’ll be visiting
is the most extreme so far.

00:15:48.360 --> 00:15:55.610
Planet Helios orbits extremely close to its
star — making it a world of blistering temperatures.

00:15:55.610 --> 00:16:00.400
Entering the atmosphere, a sun-scorched landscape
of ash and smoke awaits.

00:16:00.400 --> 00:16:01.560
Aside from the sun’s rays, endless geothermal
activity means Planet Helios is a domain of

00:16:01.560 --> 00:16:02.560
fire.

00:16:02.560 --> 00:16:06.490
Only a few barren plants are able to take
root in this infernal biosphere.

00:16:06.490 --> 00:16:10.140
I don’t expect to find much life here…

00:16:10.140 --> 00:16:13.380
But life is nothing if not stubborn.

00:16:13.380 --> 00:16:17.209
A few persistent organisms have risen to the
challenge.

00:16:17.209 --> 00:16:22.860
The most tenacious creatures we’ll call
Igneobeasts, lifeforms with a curious defense

00:16:22.860 --> 00:16:24.470
against the heat.

00:16:24.470 --> 00:16:29.649
Like the Sea Pangolins of the Indian Ocean,
they incorporate mineral-sulfides into their

00:16:29.649 --> 00:16:36.190
hide like a kind of fire-resistant second-skin
— allowing Igneobeasts to survive in this

00:16:36.190 --> 00:16:38.100
volcanic wasteland.

00:16:38.100 --> 00:16:43.870
Igneobeasts are also excellent tunnellers,
allowing them to dig for edible roots and

00:16:43.870 --> 00:16:45.060
underground aquifers.

00:16:45.060 --> 00:16:50.490
It’s an extreme life — but a life all
the same.

00:16:50.490 --> 00:16:54.520
And speaking of extreme...

00:16:54.520 --> 00:17:00.800
That was a Heliostrider, a titanic lifeform
with a body-plan unlike any organism I’ve

00:17:00.800 --> 00:17:02.230
ever come across.

00:17:02.230 --> 00:17:08.240
Their alarming height means that Heliostriders
pay little mind to anything on this planet’s

00:17:08.240 --> 00:17:09.240
surface.

00:17:09.240 --> 00:17:14.980
Like Igneobeasts, these omnivores feed primarily
on buried plant matter, sticking their heads

00:17:14.980 --> 00:17:16.730
underground to get a meal.

00:17:16.730 --> 00:17:22.309
It’s a somewhat awkward behavior that calls
to mind how ostriches look when burying their

00:17:22.309 --> 00:17:23.740
eggs.

00:17:23.740 --> 00:17:29.799
Like most lifeforms on planet Helios, the
skin of the Heliostrider seems highly resistant

00:17:29.799 --> 00:17:31.850
to the merciless solar radiation.

00:17:31.850 --> 00:17:37.220
In fact, the Heliostriders are practically
thriving.

00:17:37.220 --> 00:17:41.720
But not all creatures on planet Helios are
quite so fortunate.

00:17:41.720 --> 00:17:48.340
Cragspines are midsized scavengers that struggle
to find sustenance in this barren environment.

00:17:48.340 --> 00:17:54.110
At a glance, they resemble the savanna foxes
of our planet, although the niche they occupy

00:17:54.110 --> 00:17:55.929
is less enviable.

00:17:55.929 --> 00:18:02.259
Cragspines live short, sweaty lives under
the uncaring sun, rarely getting enough food

00:18:02.259 --> 00:18:03.950
to stave off hunger.

00:18:03.950 --> 00:18:07.480
A harsh existence for a harsh world.

00:18:07.480 --> 00:18:11.409
Planet Helios has shown us the incredible
tenacity of life.

00:18:11.409 --> 00:18:16.320
But now let’s head to our final planet...

00:18:16.320 --> 00:18:22.840
At the end of our journey through the stars,
the massive, mysterious planet Erebus awaits.

00:18:22.840 --> 00:18:27.539
From our current position, it’s impossible
to tell what the surface looks like.

00:18:27.539 --> 00:18:34.090
Entering the atmosphere and flying below the
clouds, I discover a hidden paradise is waiting.

00:18:34.090 --> 00:18:40.169
With a temperate climate and abundant vegetation,
Planet Erebus is a place where evolution is

00:18:40.169 --> 00:18:43.350
free to experiment like never before.

00:18:43.350 --> 00:18:46.860
And here is one such experiment now.

00:18:46.860 --> 00:18:48.610
My goodness.

00:18:48.610 --> 00:18:52.499
This is a, well, it’s difficult to think
of a name.

00:18:52.499 --> 00:18:54.070
I’ll call them…

00:18:54.070 --> 00:18:55.070
George.

00:18:55.070 --> 00:19:01.269
The Georges appear to be herbivores, with
mouths located on the underside of their bodies.

00:19:01.269 --> 00:19:07.570
To feed, Georges simply move across the rich
carpet of vegetation that covers the planet,

00:19:07.570 --> 00:19:10.509
essentially functioning like living lawnmowers.

00:19:10.509 --> 00:19:15.730
Their system of locomotion is comparable to
terrestrial snails — which slowly propel

00:19:15.730 --> 00:19:19.210
themselves using waves of muscular contractions.

00:19:19.210 --> 00:19:25.789
Dozens of primitive eyes cover the front end
of the Georges, which might seem like overkill.

00:19:25.789 --> 00:19:31.429
Yet certain types of scallops on our own planet
are known to have an even higher total eye-count.

00:19:31.429 --> 00:19:37.610
It’s easy to dismiss the Georges as some
sort of biological misadventure.

00:19:37.610 --> 00:19:44.140
Yet all forms of life, no matter how bizarre,
are their own sort of miracle.

00:19:44.140 --> 00:19:50.299
In the skies above Planet Erebus, something
remarkable is passing overhead.

00:19:50.299 --> 00:19:54.590
These are Areoderms, a remarkable form of
floating life.

00:19:54.590 --> 00:19:58.360
They’re held aloft by some kind of internal
gas-bladder.

00:19:58.360 --> 00:20:02.620
Their trunks aren’t quite as impressive
as those of an elephant, and are perhaps more

00:20:02.620 --> 00:20:06.149
analogous to the short prehensile noses of
Tapirs.

00:20:06.149 --> 00:20:12.220
Yet Areoderms can feed on the tops of trees,
as their buoyant bodies mean nothing is out

00:20:12.220 --> 00:20:14.429
of reach...

00:20:14.429 --> 00:20:20.210
For the last leg of my journey across Planet
Erebus, I’ll be using a special research

00:20:20.210 --> 00:20:21.460
vehicle.

00:20:21.460 --> 00:20:27.470
This exocraft is called a Minotaur — a robust
mechanical walker that allows me to travel

00:20:27.470 --> 00:20:29.510
further and stay safer.

00:20:29.510 --> 00:20:34.399
And it seems the extra precautions weren’t
a mistake.

00:20:34.399 --> 00:20:40.239
A horrifying mass of tentacles burst from
the ground, grasping at the air close to me.

00:20:40.239 --> 00:20:46.619
This subterranean terror cannot be seen in
full, so I’ll simply call them Nightmares.

00:20:46.619 --> 00:20:51.029
Without being able to see the rest of these
lifeforms, it is impossible to determine their

00:20:51.029 --> 00:20:56.859
exact biology — but they seem to be ambush
predators that burrow under their targets

00:20:56.859 --> 00:20:58.799
and attack from below.

00:20:58.799 --> 00:21:02.669
Even a paradise like Erebus can be a dangerous
place to linger...

00:21:02.669 --> 00:21:10.249
Yet with the night, a deep calm settles over
this normally restless planet.

00:21:10.249 --> 00:21:16.179
It is a bittersweet feeling to know our voyage
is complete, but the day has come to head

00:21:16.179 --> 00:21:18.840
home…

00:21:18.840 --> 00:21:23.159
Yet of course, No Man’s Sky is a game without
an end.

00:21:23.159 --> 00:21:28.830
Beyond each horizon is another one — each
planet is just a fleeting waypoint in a voyage

00:21:28.830 --> 00:21:30.570
without a destination.

00:21:30.570 --> 00:21:36.409
There’s a certain melancholy the game can
evoke, as the sheer size of the universe creeps

00:21:36.409 --> 00:21:38.059
into one’s mind.

00:21:38.059 --> 00:21:44.350
No matter how long you spend in this universe,
on a cosmic scale, there is only much a single

00:21:44.350 --> 00:21:46.379
entity can do and see.

00:21:46.379 --> 00:21:52.399
I think the team behind this project have
done an admirable job expanding this procedural

00:21:52.399 --> 00:21:53.399
universe.

00:21:53.399 --> 00:21:56.480
And I’m excited to know what comes next.

00:21:56.480 --> 00:22:00.500
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:22:00.500 --> 00:22:05.369
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support by liking, subscribing, and hitting

00:22:05.369 --> 00:22:10.090
the notification icon to stay up to date on
all things Curious.

00:22:10.090 --> 00:22:19.409
See you in the next video.

